<p>Electronic City, in south-east Bangalore along Hosur Road, grew around its IT parks and now holds tens of thousands of apartments in large gated communities, from Phase 1 and Phase 2 out towards Hebbagodi, Bommasandra and Chandapura. Home interiors here follow the housing: mostly 2 and 3 BHK flats, many bought by first-time owners and many let out to people working nearby. This guide covers what interior design in Electronic City usually involves, the local conditions that should change your specification, and a plan from handover to move-in.</p>

<h2>What homes in Electronic City usually need</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>New apartments at handover.</strong> Builders usually leave a kitchen platform, a sink and basic electrical points. Interiors add the modular kitchen and lofts, wardrobes, a TV unit, a foyer unit, false ceilings, lighting, painting and curtains. Decide early what builder work to keep, because tearing it out costs time and money.</li>
  <li><strong>Rental flats.</strong> A smaller, sturdier scope: kitchen, wardrobes in every bedroom, fans, lights and curtains. Neutral laminates and branded hinges last through several tenants.</li>
  <li><strong>Resale flats.</strong> Often a part-renovation: a new kitchen, refreshed wardrobes and rewiring of the living room. Check the plumbing and waterproofing before the woodwork goes in.</li>
  <li><strong>Villas and row houses</strong> in the communities off Hosur Road and Anekal Road need the full plan, often with staircase, utility and terrace work.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Local conditions that change the specification</h2>
<h3>Dust from the highway and construction</h3>
<p>Hosur Road traffic and the steady construction of new towers bring fine dust indoors. Prefer closed storage over open shelves, sealed edge banding on every board, and matt or anti-fingerprint laminates that wipe clean. Ask for door brushes or gaskets on wardrobes that face balconies.</p>
<h3>Heat and west sun</h3>
<p>Summers are hot and many towers have west-facing balconies. Leave an air gap behind wardrobes on external walls, choose light, UV-stable finishes for sunny rooms and plan curtains or blinds with the false ceiling, not after it.</p>
<h3>Monsoon humidity</h3>
<p>From June to October the air is humid. Use BWP (IS:710) plywood for the sink and dishwasher units and bathroom vanities, and keep boards stored indoors and off the floor during the work. The <a href="/compare/bwp-vs-bwr-vs-mr-plywood/">BWP, BWR and MR plywood comparison</a> explains the grades.</p>
<h3>Society rules</h3>
<p>Most associations set work hours, noise limits, service-lift slots and a refundable deposit, and many stop work on Sundays. A firm that already works in your community will know the rules; ask for its plan around them in writing.</p>

<h2>A plan from handover to move-in</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><small>Week 1</small><strong>Measure and brief</strong>Measure every wall, note the electrical points and write a one-page brief for each room: storage, appliances and must-haves.</li>
  <li><small>Week 1–2</small><strong>Price the list</strong>Build the list in our <a href="/calculators/home-interior-quote/">interior cost calculator</a> with the board, shutter, finish and fittings you want.</li>
  <li><small>Week 2–3</small><strong>Compare three quotes</strong>Give the same list to three firms, visit one finished home each and compare line by line.</li>
  <li><small>Week 3–4</small><strong>Design and sign off</strong>Approve drawings, the electrical layout and the colour board before production starts.</li>
  <li><small>Weeks 5–12</small><strong>Make and install</strong>Civil and electrical changes first, then false ceiling, painting, woodwork installation and a final coat.</li>
  <li><small>Handover</small><strong>Snag list</strong>Check every shutter, drawer and hinge, the sink area and the electrical points before the last payment.</li>
</ol>

